}

The posterior and the future ROMIs (each one the curve of a posterior
sample plus normal noise with that sample's \code{sigma}) are sampled by
Markov chain Monte Carlo. Forecast ROMIs outside \code{limits} are set to the
nearest limit (the paper uses 0.005 and 0.035: mortality declines by at
least 0.5\% and at most 3.5\% a year; it excludes implausible forecasts "by
setting thresholds", which this package reads as setting the values to
the thresholds). Death rates follow from the last
smoothed rates of the base period by \code{m[x, t] = m[x, t - 1] (1 - rho[x, t - 1])} (equation 20). With \code{draws = "quantiles"} (the paper's method)
draw \code{j} of \code{n} applies the quantile \code{(j - 0.5) / n} of the forecast ROMIs
